{"conjecture": "As test suites grow alongside an implementation, they encode the system's accidental structure—specific call sequences, private object states, and incidental return formats—as required behavior. A valid refactoring that alters these implementation details while preserving the public contract will fail the tests, creating a false negative signal indistinguishable from a real regression. Consequently, developers abandon correct refactorings because the test suite, having overfit to the historical implementation, provides no mechanism to distinguish between accidental coupling and genuine specification violation."}
